Dear La Monte,

Thank you for your thoughtful feedback and for purchasing a Commercial
licence! It=E2=80=99s exciting to hear about your animation desk and how yo=
u=E2=80=99re
helping artists continue working with their preferred physical materials.

I=E2=80=99m Nassim, the founder of CalibrX and a computer vision engineer.
Previously, I was the Computer Vision and Machine Learning Lead at Sparrow
Computing in the US. I recently launched CalibrX as a bootstrapped business
and am continuously improving the platform, so feedback from people using
it in real projects is especially valuable.

*Regarding team access*, shared workspaces and member invitations aren=E2=
=80=99t
supported yet. However, I=E2=80=99d be happy to activate the same Commercia=
l
licence for your collaborator at no additional cost. Once he creates an
account, please send me the email address he registered with, and I=E2=80=
=99ll
arrange that so you can each use your own login.

*For the generated targets*, you=E2=80=99re welcome to publish them with th=
e
CalibrX branding intact or remove it=E2=80=94either is fine with me. An
acknowledgment in your documentation is appreciated but not required. I
don=E2=80=99t claim ownership of the underlying ChArUco pattern; my contrib=
ution
through CalibrX is to make camera calibration accessible through a quick,
clear workflow.

Thank you also for the suggestion about separate X and Y dimensions. I
appreciate you taking the time to explain the printing anisotropy and how
you already account for it in your software. This is a useful improvement,
and I=E2=80=99ll start working on it over the next few days.

*I=E2=80=99d also love to learn more about your animation desk and the comp=
uter
vision challenges involved. If you=E2=80=99re open to a short conversation,=
 I=E2=80=99d
enjoy hearing about your setup and exploring where my experience might be
useful.*

Best regards,
Nassim
Founder, CalibrX

On Mon, Sep 21, 2026 at 5:52=E2=80=AFAM La Monte H. P. Yarroll <
piggy.yarroll@gmail.com> wrote:

> Thank you very much for the ChArUco targets! I needed one for a
> calibration project and was pleased to find your web site.
>
> We're creating an AI-enabled traditional 2D animation desk that allows
> artists to work with their preferred art supplies instead of working
> digitally. We're very early days right now--there are only two of us, a
> professional animator and an AI expert (me). Since we eventually want to
> sell calibrated cameras, I bought a Commercial license for <
> piggy.yarroll+calibrx@gmail.com>.
>
> Accounts
> How do I add my collaborator so that he can use your service too? Do we
> just share the one credential?
>
> A Legal Question
> I really appreciate your generous delegation of ownership for generated
> materials! We are licensing our calibrations and targets as CC-BY-SA-4.0.
>
> One concern I have is that the targets are calibrx.io branded, a
> trademark for which I do not hold a license. Is it OK to just acknowledge
> the trademark in our documentation or should I scrub it from the template=
s
> I publish?
>
> My Technical Request
> My printer has a small but measurable anisotropy, so my target is not 100=
%
> square (+0.15%). It would be great to provide both x and y for the square=
s
> and the images so that an anisotropic target can be accounted for. My own
> software accounts for this so I'm good to go, but I think providing those
> dimensions would be a valuable addition to your tool.
>
>

